      "Seven lookups asked the database for one person and handed it a wildcard pattern instead of a name. `.ilike(\"email\", email)` is not a case-insensitive equals — it is a LIKE match, so every metacharacter in the caller's own input gets interpreted. Reproduced against live production: a lookup for j_intwan@yahoo.com returned two rows belonging to a different address, j.intwan@yahoo.com, because `_` matches any character. A lookup for `%` returned all 593 profiles. Two of the seven were the duplicate-account checks in add-agent and create-new-agent-account, so on any email that already had two profile rows the guard against duplicates returned \"no match\" and the function created a third.",
    detail:
      "The wildcard is only half of it: two matches then collapse to null through .maybeSingle(), which is indistinguishable from zero matches, so both failure modes land on the same `if (!existing)` branch that means \"nobody has this email, go create one.\" That is self-amplifying — every duplicate it created made the next collision likelier — and profiles.email carries no unique index, with 8 colliding keys across 16 rows live, including info@kingofsales.net shared by two different people. Every number was re-measured under each site's own predicate rather than inherited: xcel-import's applications lookup collides on 40 rows / 16 keys under its real terminated_at IS NULL filter (only one key dropped out), so an Xcel licensing import could never advance those applicants and silently counted them as \"unmatched\" — the run now reports ambiguous as its own outcome instead of folding it into a clean number. The other two were measured at zero live impact and are reported that way rather than dressed up: agents.agent_code and email_unsubscribes.email are both uniquely indexed, which is exactly why the pattern match was easy to miss, since a unique index does not bound a LIKE. Escaping was proven through the PostgREST layer before being designed around, and it needs four metacharacters, not two — PostgREST rewrites * to % before the pattern reaches SQL, so a bare * also returned all 593 rows. Sweeping the same disease one layer out found five listUsers() callers scanning a single page and calling it the table: create-agent-from-leaderboard used the SDK default of 50 and consume-invite-token used 200, against 531 real auth users, so both were already wrong rather than approaching a ceiling, and consume-invite-token carried a comment reading \"small-N lookup is fine while we're early\" that was true when written and false now. All five now page to exhaustion, and a failed lookup is treated as unknown instead of as \"no such user,\" which had turned a transient auth outage into a duplicate account. The last correction was to the guard shipped this morning: it scanned raw source, so the phrase \".maybeSingle()\" written in a code comment counted as a call site. Three such comments existed, one inflating unsafe and two inflating safe, meaning the locked baseline of 82 was never real — the honest figure was 81. Left alone it would have compounded, because every wave documents the site it just converted, trading a real violation for a phantom and holding the number flat while the code genuinely improved. The guard now strips comments before scanning and is mutation-proven both ways: prose moves nothing, a real new violation still goes red.",
