      "Found by finally reading error_logs, which had been collecting since 2026 \u2014 1,049 rows, 74 in the trailing 30 days, newest three hours before this commit \u2014 while nothing in the product ever read it and nothing alerted on it. Collection without surfacing is not monitoring; it is a table that grows. Reproduced the cause directly rather than inferring it: requesting /assets/SessionWarningDialog-DEADBEEF.js returns HTTP 200, content-type text/html, with the full index.html body, while a real chunk returns application/javascript. This is the same root fact recorded at the top of this session's audit \u2014 every URL on the domain 200s, including garbage ones \u2014 which was originally noted as a monitoring blind spot and turns out to also be an active crash source. New view v_client_error_summary classifies before it counts, because the 74 rows are not one population: 57 hits across 21 assets are stale-deploy chunk misses from clients holding a previous build's index.html, which chunkRecovery.ts already self-heals and which must never look like an incident, versus 17 hits across 3 genuine crashes. Folding those together would produce a red number that is mostly noise, and a panel whose red is usually wrong gets ignored \u2014 exactly how the cron gate earned 36 false pages a day in the same week. New ClientErrorsPanel on /dashboard/system-health renders the crashes with hit counts, affected users and last-seen, keeps the stale count as a one-line footnote, and distinguishes a failed query from an empty result so it can never render \"no crashes\" because it could not read them. Remaining known crash: React error #310 (more hooks rendered than the previous render) on /dashboard/call-center, 13 hits across 2 real users, last seen 2026-08-03 and not since \u2014 left un-chased rather than guessed at, and now visible on the panel if it recurs.",
