      "wave-alert-means-what-it-says: the sync alarm was telling the truth about the wrong thing. Since this morning it went off whenever the most recent sync attempt had died — and it phrased that as \"the pipelines are not moving.\" That sentence was false almost every time it was sent. AgentLink's own servers hang on roughly one pull in four, mostly during their business hours, and one hung pull inside a healthy stream is weather, not an outage. Measured at the two moments the alarm actually woke Sam last night: the book had been refreshed 58 minutes and 21 minutes earlier, and over the previous two days a hundred successful syncs had updated 120,236 deals. It was on course for about 39 alarms a day — the same daily volume as the false alarms this morning's fix was written to kill. A true alarm nobody believes is worth exactly as much as a false one. So the alarm now measures the thing it claims to measure: how long since the book was last successfully refreshed. Any single failed pull is ignored and logged. If four hours pass with no successful sync at all, it goes red and pushes to Sam's phone — and the push now states the measured staleness instead of asserting a cause.",
    detail:
      "Third consecutive wave on this one gate, third distinct failure mode, and this one was only visible because the previous two made the gate honest. a3fa21b4's verdict was correct at the ROW level and wrong at the SYSTEM level: 9 reds in 39 runs over 5.5h (~39 priority-5 pages/day) reading 'pg_cron's backup is not moving the pipelines'. Counterfactual MEASURED, not asserted: at the ticks that paged (runs 31446750097/31443587597 ~23:48Z and 31441868477 ~00:14Z) the book's last successful refresh was 3500s and 1235s earlier — both green under the new gate. THRESHOLD RE-MEASURED AND THE PREVIOUS WAVE'S OPERAND FALSIFIED: that wave sized STALE_MAX on a 48h window and claimed max ok-to-ok gap 9981s, concluding 14400s was 'above observed-worst so throttling can never trip it'. Over 7 days and 352 consecutive ok-to-ok gaps the real distribution is p50 765s, p90 4124s, p99 11417s, max 31913s — the max EXCEEDS 14400 and the bound will fire roughly once a week. Kept at 14400 anyway, with that stated honestly in the file: it sits above p99, and the one 7d gap that crossed it (08-06 15:37 -> 08-07 00:29, 8.9h) deserved to fire — nothing asked the pipeline to sync for 7 hours, zero rows between 15:34 and 22:31. Tightening to 2h would buy 13 pages/week of noise for no extra detection. Root cause of the stuck rows is upstream and unfixable from here: every one carries upstream_status=NULL, policies_seen=0, dies in the 'deals' phase, and tracks AgentLink's business hours (44.2% stuck 13-23 UTC vs 24.2% 00-12 UTC). PREVENTION, every rule proven to fail pre-fix and pass post-fix by swapping the a3fa21b4 file in and out: check-cron-verdict-states.mjs rewritten from 18 last-settled-row states to 24 freshness states — including four REAL measured pairs (3500:stuck, 1235:stuck, 4022:stuck, 100:stuck) that were pages yesterday and are green today, and four differing only in last_status to hold the line that a diagnostic field can never vote; proven to catch a mutant that reintroduces the disease (8 states flip RED). Plus check-sync-reliability.mjs 16->17 rules: the verdict must measure max(COALESCE(finished_at,started_at)) over status='ok', last_status must not appear in the acquit test, and the push must carry the measured reason instead of the old sentence. Also corrected the workflow header, which claimed 'every 10 minutes' — measured cadence is p90 40.8min, max 108.2min, with 34 of 119 gaps over 10 minutes, and anything sizing a staleness bound off this heartbeat needs the tail, not the median. Stated limit: this gate can only speak on ticks that actually run, so it could not have paged during the 7h hole above. YAML re-parsed (4 steps), bash -n clean, live query shape verified against bot-sql (returned 4325:stuck — the exact acquit-worthy state this wave exists for).",
