      "wave-cookie-sync-red-path: this morning's fix stopped the false alarms. It also, without anyone noticing, switched the alarm off. To decide whether a sync worked, the workflow was asking the database about the sync it had started ninety seconds earlier — a run that by definition had not finished yet. The database's honest answer was always \"still running,\" and the workflow read that as \"fine.\" So every tick passed, forever, no matter what was actually happening to the pipeline. It is not a theory: at 17:22 today a sync died and was marked dead by the database five minutes later, and the run watching it reported success anyway, as did the four runs after it. The workflow now asks about the last sync that has actually finished, so the answer is a real verdict instead of a shrug. Green means the last completed sync worked. Anything else — dead, errored, no cookie, hung past the point the database gives up on it, or no sync on record at all — turns the run red and pushes to Sam's phone. A silent pipeline now also counts as a failure: if syncs simply stop happening for four hours, the run goes red rather than coasting on an old success.",
    detail:
      "Resumed from the open finding this morning's wave logged against itself. The hole was proven in production before touching anything, not hypothesised: run 31413717796 fired agentlink-cookie-sync at 17:22:53Z, hit Supabase's gateway idle timeout at 150s ({\"code\":\"IDLE_TIMEOUT\"}), logged \"verdict: status=running age=151s\" and concluded SUCCESS — while the very row it judged (ab407e97, started 17:22:54Z) ran 485s and was reaped 'stuck'. Runs at 17:24:03/05/09 and 17:27:08 also reported success. Root cause: the verdict query was `WHERE started_at >= to_timestamp($TICK_START)`, so it could only ever see the row that tick had just created; the gateway forces the verdict at ~151s and fn_agentlink_reap_stuck (INTERVAL '5 minutes', read live from pg_get_functiondef) does not declare a run stuck until 300s. Always 'running', always under threshold, always acquitted — the stuck / past-threshold / no-row branches were unreachable code. Fix: judge the newest row OLDER than the reap threshold, whose status the reaper has already settled, with the threshold extracted live from the reaper's own body via regexp_match(pg_get_functiondef(...)) so curl and the reaper cannot drift apart if anyone retunes it. Only 'ok' acquits, and only inside STALE_MAX=14400s — row-to-row gaps measured over 3 days are p50 574s, p90 1972s, p99 4707s, max 6490s, so 14400 is 2.2x the observed worst case and GitHub's schedule throttling can never trip it while four hours of silence always does. Non-numeric ages now coerce to a very-stale sentinel instead of 0; the old coercion made a garbled response ACQUIT a stale 'ok', fail-open in the one place whose job is to fail loud. Also corrects this morning's commit message: the --max-time 90->300 bump it led with is largely inert, because the gateway caps the request at 150s regardless — the real fix was and is \"the log is the verdict.\" Verified against real data rather than asserted: the new query run as-of the 18:08:18Z tick returns status=stuck age=2723, so that run — which actually reported SUCCESS — goes RED under the new gate. Prevention, both proven to fail pre-fix and pass post-fix: new scripts/check-cron-verdict-states.mjs extracts the SHIPPED bash decision block out of the workflow and executes it through 18 verdict states (old block fails 5, new passes 18/18), wired into verify:core and gated in .husky/pre-commit; plus 4 new static rules in check-sync-reliability.mjs (16 total) banning a current-tick anchor, requiring the live reap threshold, requiring a stale-ok bound, and banning the fail-open age coercion. YAML re-parsed and bash -n clean on the extracted step.",
