    label:
      "wave-cron-tick-fake-failure: yesterday's fix made the sync-backup workflow's green honest. Today it started crying wolf. The alarm fired 36 times in 19 hours saying \"the pipelines are not moving\" — and a large share of those were lies. The AgentLink sync was being hung up on after 90 seconds, but its successful runs take a median of 143 seconds and up to 192. So half of all SUCCESSFUL syncs were being cut off mid-call, recorded as a connection failure, and turned into a red run plus a priority-5 push to Sam's phone — while the sync it had hung up on went on to finish and update 1202 deals. That is the same fake-success disease this codebase keeps finding, running in reverse: a fake FAILURE. It is just as expensive, because an alarm that is wrong a dozen times a day teaches Sam to swipe it away, and the next one will be real. The call now waits the full 5 minutes, and when it does give up, the run's verdict is read from the sync's own database record instead of from how long the network call was willing to wait. Green means the pipelines moved. Red means they didn't. Both are now decided by the same source of truth.",
    detail:
      "Resumed and closed wave-cron-tick-honest-green first: marker /tmp/wib-honest-green.status read PUSHED 710b27c3 0/0, confirmed on origin/main with a clean tree, so that wave is done. Verifying it under live traffic is what surfaced this. 100 runs since it landed: 64 success / 36 failure, all on the same SHA — a gate flapping on one commit. Root cause measured, not guessed: 56 agentlink_sync_log rows over 20h, 40 ok / 16 stuck; of the 40 SUCCESSFUL syncs, 20 (exactly 50%) ran longer than call_edge's hardcoded `--max-time 90` — median 143s, p90 177s, max 192s. Correlated one red run end-to-end to prove it rather than inferring: GH run 31399414256 logged `edge agentlink-cookie-sync returned status=000` at 14:41:57Z after exactly 90s, while the log row started 14:40:28Z finished 14:43:24Z with status=ok and deals_updated=1202. The workflow called a success a failure and paged Sam about it. Fix in .github/workflows/external-cron-backup.yml: call_edge takes a timeout arg (default unchanged at 90) and agentlink-cookie-sync gets 300 — not an arbitrary bump but exactly the 5-minute threshold fn_agentlink_reap_stuck already uses to declare a run stuck, read live from pg_get_functiondef, so curl and the reaper share one definition of \"too long\" and cannot disagree about the same run. If curl still gives up, the verdict is reconciled against agentlink_sync_log: 'ok' acquits, 'running' under the 300s reap threshold acquits (still legitimately in flight; the next tick judges it), and 'stuck', past-threshold, or no row at all stays red. Proven before commit across 11 states under `bash -e -u -o pipefail` — slow-but-successful green, in-flight green, past-reap red, stuck red, no-log-row red — including three cases proving the acquittal cannot widen: a healthy sync alongside another failed job still goes red. jq verdict parser tested against 6 response shapes (healthy, stuck, empty rows, ok:false, an HTML login page, empty body); the empty-body case makes jq exit 0 with no output so the `||` fallback never fires, which already failed safe to red but now names itself 'unknown' instead of a blank. YAML re-parsed (4 steps intact) and bash -n clean after every edit. Prevention: 2 new rules in scripts/check-sync-reliability.mjs (12 rules total, already gated on this workflow in .husky/pre-commit) — the cookie-sync call must carry an explicit >=300s timeout, and a timeout must be reconciled against agentlink_sync_log before counting as a failure. Both proven to FAIL against the pre-fix file and pass against the fix, run both ways. Separately logged, not fixed here: GitHub throttles these scheduled runs hard — observed gaps of 32, 38 and 41 minutes against a stated ~1-minute cadence, so the header's \"every 10 minutes\" promise is not being met by GH's scheduler.",
